 pardon. Or a future.\n\nInland: jungle, then ruins, then the cave. a cursed doubloon's X marked a chamber sealed a century. Inside: not gold — at first. A tomb. A logbook. The real a cursed doubloon: the captain's confession, naming every cache along Smuggler's Cove.\n\nThe point arrived with the cave's guardians — the island's eaters, whatever they were, stone and shadow. In the chaos Captain Jack Thorne seized a cursed doubloon, the navigator seized the gold, and they ran for the ship with the dark at their heels."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "the navigator fought like three people. The crew fought like the sea itself. Captain Jack Thorne faced the Spanish governor with cutlasses and old scores, and the duel was the kind they sing about: give, take, blood in the sand, neither yielding.\n\nIt ended as these things do: the Spanish governor overreached, Captain Jack Thorne didn't, and the sea took the Spanish governor with the tide. Captain Jack Thorne stood in the surf, breathing hard, a cursed doubloon safe. 'Bury them decent,' Captain Jack Thorne ordered. Pirates have rules.\n\nThey made the ship with the tide and the devils both rising. the Spanish governor made it too — of course; villains are buoyant. The final fight was on the beach, in the surf, with a cursed doubloon in Captain Jack Thorne's coat and everything to lose."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Port and Pardon', 'text': "Some of the crew shipped out again, chasing the logbook's caches. Captain Jack Thorne stayed — for a season. The sea calls, and Captain Jack Thorne always answers. But home, Captain Jack Thorne was learning, is also an answer.\n\nSmuggler's Cove welcomed them with bells and rum. The gold was divided fair — the articles are the articles. a cursed doubloon's copies went to every crew member who'd earned one. The original stayed with Captain Jack Thorne.\n\nCaptain Jack Thorne took the logbook to the governor — not the Spanish governor's governor, the new one — and traded certain pages for a pardon, signed and sealed. The crew cheered. Respectability, it turns out, is its own adventure."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Tide Returns', 'text': "A year later, a new a cursed doubloon surfaced — they always do — and Captain Jack Thorne was packed before the ink dried. the navigator closed the tavern for 'renovations' and came along, because that's what friends do.\n\nfortune favors the bold. Full sail. The horizon, as always, was just the beginning.\n\nThe new crew was young and wide-eyed. Captain Jack Thorne read them the articles, including the added one. They cheered, the way crews do. Pirates are sentimentalists. It's why they survive."}]}

